::ENG1_U04_L03_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Source sentence: Students who had access to quiet rooms after school reported fewer distractions during homework time. Which note is best?
{
=A. quiet rooms may reduce homework distractions #Correct. This is short, useful, and accurate.
~B. Students who had access to quiet rooms after school reported fewer distractions during homework time. #Incorrect. This copies the source sentence instead of taking a note.
~C. Quiet rooms make all homework perfect. #Incorrect. This exaggerates the source.
~D. After school is a time of day. #Incorrect. This is true but not the useful source finding.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Prompt: Source sentence: Students who attended peer tutoring regularly said they felt more comfortable asking questions in class. Which paraphrase is most accurate?
{
~A. Peer tutoring makes every student speak in every class. #Incorrect. This overstates the source.
~B. Students never ask questions after tutoring. #Incorrect. This reverses the source meaning.
~C. Peer tutoring is held in a classroom. #Incorrect. This detail is not in the source sentence.
=D. Students who often went to peer tutoring felt more confident asking class questions. #Correct. This restates the meaning accurately in new wording.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Source A says later start times may help teens get more sleep. Source B says students report feeling more alert when school starts later. Which synthesis is best?
{
=A. Both sources suggest later start times may support teen rest and alertness. #Correct. This combines the related findings clearly.
~B. Source A and Source B are both sources. #Incorrect. This does not connect the findings.
~C. Later start times are times that are later. #Incorrect. This repeats words instead of synthesizing.
~D. Sleep and school are unrelated topics. #Incorrect. The sources connect sleep and school start times.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Research question: How can school gardens support biology learning? Which note is most useful?
{
~A. Gardens can have fences. #Incorrect. This may be true but does not answer the biology learning question.
~B. Some gardens are outside. #Incorrect. This is too general to be useful.
=C. Garden activities can help students observe plant growth directly. #Correct. This note connects the garden to biology learning.
~D. Students sometimes walk near gardens. #Incorrect. This does not connect to learning.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Prompt: Source sentence: A school survey found that students were more likely to see announcements when they appeared on classroom screens. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. Students never notice announcements anywhere. #Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
=B. Students noticed announcements more often when they were shown on classroom screens. #Correct. This keeps the meaning and changes the wording.
~C. Classroom screens are always better than teachers. #Incorrect. This adds unsupported comparison.
~D. Announcements are not important for students. #Incorrect. This does not match the source.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q06::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Which note is too vague to be useful?
{
~A. weekly tutoring linked to stronger quiz confidence #Incorrect. This is a useful focused note.
~B. digital announcements helped students notice club deadlines #Incorrect. This is specific and useful.
~C. quiet study spaces reduced reported distractions #Incorrect. This note is specific and useful.
=D. stuff happened at school #Correct. This note is too vague to help answer a research question.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q07::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Source A says students need quiet space before school. Source B says the library is available before school twice a week. Which synthesis is strongest?
{
~A. Source A says one thing and Source B says another thing. #Incorrect. This does not connect the findings.
~B. Libraries are rooms that contain books. #Incorrect. This is background, not synthesis.
=C. The library's before-school hours may partly meet students' need for quiet workspace. #Correct. This combines need and available access.
~D. Students are people who attend school. #Incorrect. This does not use the source findings.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q08::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Prompt: Which sentence uses clear standard English for a research note?
{
=A. Students who attended tutoring reported stronger quiz confidence. #Correct. This sentence is clear and grammatically correct.
~B. Students, tutoring, quiz, confidence, good. #Incorrect. This is a fragment list and is not clear.
~C. Tutoring help student they quiz better. #Incorrect. This sentence has grammar errors that make meaning unclear.
~D. Students is helped by tutoring every times. #Incorrect. The sentence has agreement and wording errors.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q09::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Research question: How can a breakfast cart affect students who arrive early? Which source finding is most useful?
{
~A. Some carts have wheels. #Incorrect. This detail does not explain student need or effect.
~B. Breakfast is a word with nine letters. #Incorrect. This does not help answer the research question.
~C. First period occurs at school. #Incorrect. This is too general to be useful.
=D. Early-arriving students often wait without eating before first period. #Correct. This finding connects directly to the breakfast cart question.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q10::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Prompt: Source sentence: Students with access to online calendars missed fewer assignment deadlines. Which paraphrase is best?
{
~A. Online calendars make deadlines disappear. #Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
=B. Students who could use online calendars missed fewer due dates. #Correct. This accurately restates the source.
~C. Students never need calendars. #Incorrect. This reverses the source meaning.
~D. Assignment deadlines are always unfair. #Incorrect. This adds opinion not in the source.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q11::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Source A says many students miss hallway flyers. Source B says digital announcements can be updated quickly. Which synthesis is strongest?
{
~A. Source A is about flyers, and Source B is about screens. #Incorrect. This names topics but does not synthesize.
~B. Flyers are paper, and screens are electronic. #Incorrect. This comparison does not explain the research connection.
=C. Digital announcements may improve school communication because they are easier to update and may reach students who miss flyers. #Correct. This connects both source findings.
~D. Announcements exist in many places. #Incorrect. This is too general.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q12::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Which action is best when taking research notes?
{
=A. Record key findings that help answer the research question. #Correct. Useful notes stay connected to the inquiry.
~B. Copy every sentence from the source. #Incorrect. Notes should be selective and often shortened.
~C. Write only opinions and ignore source evidence. #Incorrect. Research notes should come from source information.
~D. Mix unrelated facts with no labels. #Incorrect. This makes later writing harder.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q13::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Prompt: Source sentence: The media center had its highest student use on days it opened before school. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. No students used the media center before school. #Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
~B. The media center was closed every morning. #Incorrect. This reverses the source.
=C. More students used the media center on mornings when it opened before classes. #Correct. This keeps the meaning in new wording.
~D. Students only use cafeterias before school. #Incorrect. This adds unsupported information.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q14::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Which synthesis sentence best combines findings about school gardens?
{
=A. School gardens can support biology learning by letting students observe plant growth and connect vocabulary to real examples. #Correct. This combines related findings into one explanation.
~B. Gardens have plants. Biology has vocabulary. #Incorrect. This lists ideas without connecting them.
~C. Plants are green sometimes. #Incorrect. This is too narrow and not synthesized.
~D. Vocabulary is in classes. #Incorrect. This does not connect the research findings.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q15::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: A student writes this note: "The source says everything about tutoring." What is the problem?
{
~A. It is too specific. #Incorrect. The note is not specific at all.
~B. It uses too much evidence. #Incorrect. It gives no clear evidence.
~C. It is a perfect paraphrase. #Incorrect. It is not an accurate or useful paraphrase.
=D. It is too vague to help answer the research question. #Correct. The note does not identify a useful finding.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q16::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Source A says students use tutoring to ask questions. Source B says students use tutoring to revise mistakes. What related idea connects both sources?
{
~A. Tutoring rooms have furniture. #Incorrect. This does not connect the findings.
=B. Tutoring gives students academic support beyond regular class time. #Correct. This idea connects both findings.
~C. Students never need help. #Incorrect. This contradicts both findings.
~D. Mistakes should be ignored. #Incorrect. This contradicts Source B.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q17::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Prompt: Source sentence: Students who ate breakfast before class reported stronger focus during first period. Which paraphrase is best?
{
~A. Breakfast makes every class easy. #Incorrect. This exaggerates the source.
=B. Students who had breakfast before school said they focused better in first period. #Correct. This accurately restates the source.
~C. Students never focus without breakfast. #Incorrect. This overstates the finding.
~D. First period is always about breakfast. #Incorrect. This adds unsupported information.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q18::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Why is synthesis more than listing notes?
{
~A. Synthesis means copying sources exactly. #Incorrect. Copying is not synthesis.
~B. Synthesis avoids the research question. #Incorrect. Synthesis should support the inquiry.
~C. Synthesis removes all source information. #Incorrect. It uses source information, but connects it.
=D. Synthesis connects related findings to build understanding. #Correct. Synthesis shows how source ideas work together.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q19::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Research question: How can lunch-time library access affect students who need quiet study space? Which note is most useful?
{
=A. Students reported using lunch-time library access to finish missed assignments. #Correct. This directly connects lunch access to student work.
~B. Lunch is served in the cafeteria. #Incorrect. This does not answer the quiet study space question.
~C. Library shelves can be tall. #Incorrect. This detail is not useful for the research question.
~D. Some students bring backpacks. #Incorrect. This is unrelated to the inquiry.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q20::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Prompt: Which sentence best presents a synthesized finding?
{
~A. Source, study, thing, homework, quiet. #Incorrect. This is not a complete clear sentence.
~B. Quiet study space help students because homework finish. #Incorrect. Grammar problems make the meaning unclear.
=C. The sources suggest that quiet study access may help students complete homework before other activities. #Correct. This is a clear synthesis sentence.
~D. Homework and media center and students before school. #Incorrect. This is a fragment list.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q21::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Which source finding would be least useful for the question "How can peer tutoring affect student confidence?"
{
~A. Students said tutoring helped them ask more questions. #Incorrect. This directly supports the research question.
~B. Students reported feeling more prepared for quizzes after tutoring. #Incorrect. This connects tutoring to confidence.
~C. Teachers noticed tutoring students revised more work. #Incorrect. This is relevant to academic support.
=D. The tutoring center door is painted blue. #Correct. This does not explain confidence or tutoring effects.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q22::[MLA.ENG1.R.10] Prompt: Source sentence: Digital announcements were updated more quickly than hallway flyers. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
=A. Digital announcements could be changed faster than printed hallway flyers. #Correct. This keeps the source meaning in new words.
~B. Hallway flyers are always better than digital announcements. #Incorrect. This reverses the comparison.
~C. Announcements never change. #Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
~D. Flyers are the only communication tool. #Incorrect. This adds unsupported information.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q23::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: What should a research note always do?
{
~A. Replace the research question. #Incorrect. Notes support the question; they do not replace it.
=B. Preserve the source meaning accurately. #Correct. Accuracy matters even when notes are brief.
~C. Copy full paragraphs exactly. #Incorrect. Notes should be selective and useful.
~D. Include unrelated personal opinions. #Incorrect. Research notes should focus on source findings.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q24::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Which pair of notes is ready for synthesis?
{
~A. media center has lights; cafeteria has trays #Incorrect. These notes are not clearly related to one inquiry.
~B. bus routes changed; gardens have soil #Incorrect. These notes are unrelated.
=C. quiet rooms reduce distractions; students finish homework earlier in quiet spaces #Correct. These related notes can be synthesized.
~D. school colors are blue; students like music #Incorrect. These notes do not support a shared research question.
}

::ENG1_U04_L03_Q25::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Which sentence best explains why paraphrase matters in research?
{
~A. Paraphrase lets writers change the source meaning. #Incorrect. Paraphrase must preserve the meaning.
=B. Paraphrase helps writers use source ideas accurately in their own words. #Correct. This is the purpose of paraphrase in research.
~C. Paraphrase means removing all source information. #Incorrect. It keeps source meaning.
~D. Paraphrase means copying every word. #Incorrect. Copying is not paraphrasing.
}
